"The oaths at the beginning of this surah are like a summary of the oaths in the previous surah. There, the path leading from darkness to light, and from non-existence to knowing Allah and the soul, was shown. Here, however, attention is drawn to darkness by swearing by the night and the moment it envelops. Then an oath is sworn by the day as it shines forth, and by the One Who created the male and the female. These oaths point from transient non-existence to permanence, unity, and the order in creation.\n\nThen it is stated, 'Indeed, your efforts are diverse.' The word 'shatta' means scattered and separate. This indicates that people's intentions and deeds differ, with some striving toward good and others toward evil. The primary objective is to caution against disunity as in the Age of Ignorance (Jahiliyyah) and to guide toward order through tawhid and action. The divergence of efforts stems from the variation of whims and desires within the souls.\n\n'As for he who gives, is mindful of Allah (has taqwa), and affirms the best (husna), We will ease him toward the easiest path.' Here, spending one's wealth in the way of Allah, guarding against evil, and believing in the finest outcome (Paradise, the pleasure of Allah) are praised. 'Husna' has been interpreted as the most excellent trait, word, or reward. With the help of Allah, such a person finds ease on the path of goodness and attains happiness.\n\n'But as for he who is stingy, considers himself self-sufficient, and denies the best, We will ease him toward the hardest path.' A person who acts stingily and does not give from his wealth, deems himself self-sufficient, and denies the Hereafter is dragged toward a harsh end. When that person perishes, his wealth will not benefit him. Amassing wealth cannot save him.\n\n'Indeed, it is incumbent upon Us to show guidance. And surely to Us belong the latter and the former.' Allah shows guidance through the prophets and the books. The Hereafter and this world belong to Him. No one is independent of His guidance. Then comes the warning of 'a blazing Fire.' Into that Fire will enter only the 'most wretched,' namely, the one who denied the Truth and turned away.\n\n'But the most righteous will be kept far away from it—the one who gives his wealth to purify himself, and harbors no favor from anyone to be repaid, seeking only the countenance of his Lord, the Most High. And he will surely be well-pleased.' It is narrated that these verses were revealed concerning Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) on account of his freeing slaves like Bilal. The most righteous person, having attained the pleasure of Allah, reaches contentment in this world and the Hereafter."